// Staff content (GAMEPLAY.md §7 "Staff — a resource pool, not NPCs" and
// §5's third cost axis, "staff wages — recurring upkeep"). Data, never
// hardcoded in sim_core, exactly like `recipes.ron`, `machines.ron` and
// `shop.ron`; the loader is `sim_core::sim::staff::StaffCatalog`.
//
// `schema_version` follows ARCHITECTURE.md §4's data-file versioning rule:
// the loader rejects a mismatched version rather than silently misreading a
// changed shape. Content changes (a wage, a headcount, a new category) never
// bump it.
//
// WHAT THIS FILE IS FOR. Staff are an aggregate capacity per CATEGORY, not
// people walking the floor (GAMEPLAY.md §7). Every machine kind in
// `data/machines.ron` declares the crew it needs *while running* from the
// categories defined here; each tick the simulation resets every category
// to its hired headcount and lets machines claim their whole crew in stable
// order, so a factory short of people has the LAST machines in that order
// standing idle (`docs/STAFF_POOLS_PLAN.md`, "Why it is shaped this way").
//
// THE FOUR CATEGORIES ARE GAMEPLAY.md §7's, deliberately not more: each one
// gates a different stage a sauce passes through, so a shortfall reads
// clearly — manufacturing idles the machines, packaging backs product up
// in front of the bottler, logistics leaves boxes standing on the dock.
// `engineering` exists with nothing to keep running yet (nothing corrodes
// until GAMEPLAY.md's tier 5), so it starts at zero and no machine asks
// for it; it is here so the panel and the save already know the shape.
//
// RENAMING A CATEGORY. Saves key the crew by category id, so a rename
// keeps the old id in `renamed_from`: a save naming it loads into the
// renamed category with its counts, and the next save writes the new id.
// `engineering` was `maintenance` until 2026-10-02. A former id may not be
// any category's live id or another category's former id — a save naming
// it must mean exactly one category. Never drop an entry: an old save
// would stop loading.
//
// ORDER MATTERS TWICE. This is the order the staff panel lists categories
// in, and it is the PAYDAY order: on payday heads are paid one at a time
// down this list, and the first head that cannot be paid — and every head
// after it — goes UNPAID. Manufacturing is first because a factory that
// must leave someone unpaid should idle its shipping clerk before its cook.
//
// WHAT AN UNPAID HEAD DOES (the quality pass, 2026-09-13; the rule is
// `sim_core/src/sim/staff.rs`'s, this is the summary). It stays hired but
// does not work the period it was not paid for, so the machines it crewed
// starve with a `staff` stall and the staff panel says how many are
// unpaid; it leaves only if the NEXT payday cannot pay it either, and a
// payday that covers it clears the arrears. Two strikes, like a contract.
// A head is owed for any period it was employed in any part of: a head
// fired before payday is still billed at that payday (notice pay, paid
// first), a head hired just before payday is billed at it, and a hire is
// refused while the balance cannot cover the next bill plus the new wage.
// So `wage` here is the cost of a head per period however the period was
// spent, and nothing the player does with HIRE and FIRE changes what a
// period's employment costs.
//
// NUMBERS. `wage` is whole money units per head per `pay_period_ticks`.
// Everything is a placeholder in the sense GAMEPLAY.md §10 means (the
// balancing pass owns the figures), but the SHAPE is deliberate:
//
//   1. The starting crew is sized to the shipped reference line — the
//      summed `staff` of its kinds in `machines.ron` (crusher 1 + mixer 1
//      + vat 2 manufacturing; bottler 1 + packer 1 packaging; dock 1
//      logistics) — the same way `STARTING_MONEY` is sized to that line's
//      bill. A first factory runs without a visit to the staff panel and
//      the SECOND line is what makes the player hire.
//      `sim_core/tests/staff.rs` pins that relationship, not the literals.
//   2. The whole crew costs 14 per 1000 ticks against the starter tier's
//      payouts, so wages are felt from the first payday without being able
//      to sink a line that is delivering. Checked against the numbers on
//      2026-09-13: one kept-fed line makes about 4 boxes per 1000 ticks
//      (`data/contracts.ron`'s balance note), which the three starter-tier
//      orders pay about 100 for, on ingredients of about 43 (4 tomato + 1
//      sugar + 2 vinegar per box, 11 a box) — roughly 60 a period clear
//      before wages, 45 after. Wages are about a quarter of a first line's
//      margin: felt, not fatal.
//   3. One flat wage: the categories differ by what they gate, not yet by
//      what they cost. A zero wage is legal content (a volunteer category).
(
    schema_version: 1,
    pay_period_ticks: 1000,
    categories: [
        StaffCategory(id: "manufacturing", name: "Manufacturing", wage: 2, starting_headcount: 4),
        StaffCategory(id: "engineering",   name: "Engineering",   wage: 2, starting_headcount: 0, renamed_from: ["maintenance"]),
        StaffCategory(id: "packaging",     name: "Packaging",     wage: 2, starting_headcount: 2),
        StaffCategory(id: "logistics",     name: "Logistics",     wage: 2, starting_headcount: 1),
    ],
)
